# Copy to /etc/btc-intel/collector.env, chmod 0640, owner root:btc-intel.
# `deploy/install.sh` does this for you.
#
# This is the collector subset, with the host guidance that belongs on a host.
# The complete repository-wide template -- the API and the research runs as well
# -- is `.env.example` at the root. A test asserts this file names nothing that
# one omits.
#
# NEVER commit a filled-in copy of this file. Nothing here is required: the
# deployment collects from public government feeds and needs no credential at
# all. That is a property worth keeping -- a collector with no secret cannot
# leak one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- where state lives
# The unit sets this to systemd's StateDirectory and you should normally leave
# it alone. It is listed because the collector refuses to start on a path it
# cannot prove is writable, and because it warns loudly if the root looks
# ephemeral: a corpus under /tmp is a corpus that ends at the next reboot, and
# the accumulated availability record cannot be re-collected.
# BTC_INTEL_STATE_ROOT=/var/lib/btc-intel

# Individual paths can be split -- a fast disk for the database, a large one for
# backups -- without choosing between them.
# BTC_INTEL_DATABASE=/var/lib/btc-intel/intelligence.duckdb
# BTC_INTEL_BACKUP_DIR=/mnt/backup/btc-intel

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- contact
# The one value you must set. It goes into the User-Agent of every feed request
# as:  BTC-Forecaster Research <this address>
#
# This is not decoration. The SEC's access policy asks automated clients to
# identify themselves with a contact and answers 403 to those that do not. One
# manual fetch slips through; a collector polling eight times a day for months
# is precisely what gets blocked -- weeks later, as a PERMANENT failure on a
# feed everyone assumed was working.
#
# Use a dedicated project address rather than a personal one. The collector
# refuses to start if the profile asks for this and it is unset, rather than
# advertising an unexpanded placeholder.
BTC_INTEL_CONTACT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- alerts
# Every unit that can fail raises btc-intel-alert@.service, which writes the
# alert to the journal at CRITICAL. To be told somewhere you will see it, give a
# command that reads the message on standard input -- mail, a webhook, anything
# the host already has. It may contain a token: this file is 0640 root:btc-intel,
# and the command is never printed or logged.
# BTC_INTEL_ALERT_COMMAND=mail -s "btc-intel alert" ops@example.org

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- logging
# Verbosity of the structured JSON log written to stderr, which the journal
# captures. DEBUG / INFO / WARNING / ERROR; INFO is the default and is what
# makes per-provider outcomes visible in `journalctl -o cat | jq`.
# BTC_INTEL_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- provenance
# Recorded in every manifest so a corpus can name the code that produced it.
# The installer sets this from the deployed checkout.
# BTC_INTEL_SOURCE_SHA=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- credentials
# Only set this if a paid contract genuinely exists. The variable being present
# does not enable the provider -- the profile has to name it too -- and an
# unset variable is reported as "not enabled", never guessed at or defaulted.
# BTC_INTEL_SEARCH_API_KEY=
